R309	Speaker Arg1:T231 Arg2:T233	
R310	Addressee Arg1:T232 Arg2:T233	
T234	Quotation 67905 67939	"Yes, indeed, a very good letter,"
T235	Cue 67940 67947	replied
T236	Entity 67948 67952	Emma
T237	Quotation 67969 68718	"so good a letter, Harriet, that every thing considered, I think one of his sisters must have helped him.  I can hardly imagine the young man whom I saw talking with you the other day could express himself so well, if left quite to his own powers, and yet it is not the style of a woman; no, certainly, it is too strong and concise; not diffuse enough for a woman.  No doubt he is a sensible man, and I suppose may have a natural talent for-- thinks strongly and clearly-- and when he takes a pen in hand, his thoughts naturally find proper words.  It is so with some men.  Yes, I understand the sort of mind.  Vigorous, decided, with sentiments to a certain point, not coarse.  A better written letter, Harriet (returning it,) than I had expected."
R311	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T234	
R312	Cueing Arg1:T235 Arg2:T234	
R313	Addressee Arg1:T231 Arg2:T234	
R314	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T237	
R315	Cueing Arg1:T235 Arg2:T237	
R316	Addressee Arg1:T231 Arg2:T237	
T238	Quotation 68719 68726	"Well,"
T239	Cue 68727 68731	said
T240	Entity 68750 68757	Harriet
T241	Quotation 68761 68796	"well-- and-- and what shall I do?"
R317	Cueing Arg1:T239 Arg2:T238	
R318	Cueing Arg1:T239 Arg2:T241	
R319	Speaker Arg1:T240 Arg2:T238	
R320	Speaker Arg1:T240 Arg2:T241	
R321	Addressee Arg1:T236 Arg2:T238	
R322	Addressee Arg1:T236 Arg2:T241	
T242	Quotation 68797 68874	"What shall you do! In what respect? Do you mean with regard to this letter?"
R323	Addressee Arg1:T240 Arg2:T242	
R324	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T242	
T243	Quotation 68875 68881	"Yes."
R325	Speaker Arg1:T240 Arg2:T243	
R326	Addressee Arg1:T236 Arg2:T243	
T244	Quotation 68882 68958	"But what are you in doubt of? You must answer it of course-- and speedily."
R327	Addressee Arg1:T240 Arg2:T244	
R328	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T244	
T245	Quotation 68959 69022	"Yes. But what shall I say? Dear Miss Woodhouse, do advise me."
R329	Speaker Arg1:T240 Arg2:T245	
R330	Addressee Arg1:T236 Arg2:T245	
T246	Quotation 69023 69519	"Oh no, no! the letter had much better be all your own.  You will express yourself very properly, I am sure.  There is no danger of your not being intelligible, which is the first thing.  Your meaning must be unequivocal; no doubts or demurs: and such expressions of gratitude and concern for the pain you are inflicting as propriety requires, will present themselves unbidden to your mind, I am persuaded.  You need not be prompted to write with the appearance of sorrow for his disappointment."
R331	Addressee Arg1:T240 Arg2:T246	
R332	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T246	
T247	Quotation 69520 69559	"You think I ought to refuse him then,"
T248	Cue 69560 69564	said
T249	Entity 69565 69572	Harriet
R333	Cueing Arg1:T248 Arg2:T247	
R334	Speaker Arg1:T249 Arg2:T247	
T250	Quotation 69588 69919	"Ought to refuse him! My dear Harriet, what do you mean? Are you in any doubt as to that? I thought-- but I beg your pardon, perhaps I have been under a mistake.  I certainly have been misunderstanding you, if you feel in doubt as to the purport of your answer.  I had imagined you were consulting me only as to the wording of it."
R335	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T250	
T251	Entity 69974 69978	Emma
R336	Speaker Arg1:T251 Arg2:T250	
T252	Cue 69979 69988	continued
T253	Quotation 69990 70042	"You mean to return a favourable answer, I collect."
R337	Speaker Arg1:T251 Arg2:T253	
R338	Cueing Arg1:T252 Arg2:T253	
R339	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T253	
T254	Quotation 70043 70187	"No, I do not; that is, I do not mean-- What shall I do? What would you advise me to do? Pray, dear Miss Woodhouse, tell me what I ought to do."
R340	Addressee Arg1:T251 Arg2:T254	
R341	Speaker Arg1:T249 Arg2:T254	
T255	Quotation 70188 70326	"I shall not give you any advice, Harriet.  I will have nothing to do with it.  This is a point which you must settle with your feelings."
R342	Speaker Arg1:T251 Arg2:T255	
R343	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T255	
T256	Quotation 70327 70375	"I had no notion that he liked me so very much,"
T257	Cue 70376 70380	said
T258	Entity 70381 70388	Harriet
R344	Cueing Arg1:T257 Arg2:T256	
R345	Speaker Arg1:T258 Arg2:T256	
T259	Entity 70436 70440	Emma
R346	Addressee Arg1:T259 Arg2:T256	
T260	Quotation 70585 71021	"I lay it down as a general rule, Harriet, that if a woman doubts as to whether she should accept a man or not, she certainly ought to refuse him.  If she can hesitate as to 'Yes,' she ought to say 'No' directly.  It is not a state to be safely entered into with doubtful feelings, with half a heart.  I thought it my duty as a friend, and older than yourself, to say thus much to you.  But do not imagine that I want to influence you."
T261	Cue 70580 70583	say
R347	Cueing Arg1:T261 Arg2:T260	
R348	Speaker Arg1:T259 Arg2:T260	
R349	Addressee Arg1:T258 Arg2:T260	
T262	Quotation 71022 71347	"Oh! no, I am sure you are a great deal too kind to-- but if you would just advise me what I had best do-- No, no, I do not mean that-- As you say, one's mind ought to be quite made up-- One should not be hesitating-- It is a very serious thing.-- It will be safer to say 'No,' perhaps.-- Do you think I had better say 'No?'"
R350	Addressee Arg1:T259 Arg2:T262	
R351	Speaker Arg1:T258 Arg2:T262	
T263	Quotation 71348 71368	"Not for the world,"
T264	Cue 71369 71373	said
T265	Entity 71374 71378	Emma
T266	Quotation 71400 71865	"would I advise you either way.  You must be the best judge of your own happiness.  If you prefer Mr. Martin to every other person; if you think him the most agreeable man you have ever been in company with, why should you hesitate? You blush, Harriet.-- Does any body else occur to you at this moment under such a definition? Harriet, Harriet, do not deceive yourself; do not be run away with by gratitude and compassion.  At this moment whom are you thinking of?"
R352	Cueing Arg1:T264 Arg2:T263	
R353	Cueing Arg1:T264 Arg2:T266	
R354	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T263	
R355	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T266	
R356	Addressee Arg1:T258 Arg2:T263	
R357	Addressee Arg1:T258 Arg2:T266	
T267	Quotation 72203 72413	"Miss Woodhouse, as you will not give me your opinion, I must do as well as I can by myself; and I have now quite determined, and really almost made up my mind-- to refuse Mr. Martin.  Do you think I am right?"
T268	Entity 72188 72195	Harriet
R358	Speaker Arg1:T268 Arg2:T267	
T269	Cue 72196 72200	said
R359	Cueing Arg1:T269 Arg2:T267	
R360	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T267	
T270	Quotation 72414 73058	"Perfectly, perfectly right, my dearest Harriet; you are doing just what you ought.  While you were at all in suspense I kept my feelings to myself, but now that you are so completely decided I have no hesitation in approving.  Dear Harriet, I give myself joy of this.  It would have grieved me to lose your acquaintance, which must have been the consequence of your marrying Mr. Martin.  While you were in the smallest degree wavering, I said nothing about it, because I would not influence; but it would have been the loss of a friend to me.  I could not have visited Mrs. Robert Martin, of Abbey-Mill Farm.  Now I am secure of you for ever."
R361	Addressee Arg1:T268 Arg2:T270	
R362	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T270	
T271	Quotation 73140 73172	"You could not have visited me!"
T272	Entity 73173 73176	she
T273	Cue 73177 73182	cried
R363	Speaker Arg1:T272 Arg2:T271	
R364	Cueing Arg1:T273 Arg2:T271	
R365	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T271	
T274	Quotation 73201 73444	"No, to be sure you could not; but I never thought of that before.  That would have been too dreadful!-- What an escape!-- Dear Miss Woodhouse, I would not give up the pleasure and honour of being intimate with you for any thing in the world."
R366	Speaker Arg1:T272 Arg2:T274	
R367	Cueing Arg1:T273 Arg2:T274	
R368	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T274	
T275	Quotation 73445 73616	"Indeed, Harriet, it would have been a severe pang to lose you; but it must have been.  You would have thrown yourself out of all good society.  I must have given you up."
R369	Addressee Arg1:T272 Arg2:T275	
R370	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T275	
T276	Quotation 73617 73723	"Dear me!-- How should I ever have borne it! It would have killed me never to come to Hartfield any more!"
R371	Speaker Arg1:T272 Arg2:T276	
R372	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T276	
T277	Quotation 73724 73974	"Dear affectionate creature!-- You banished to Abbey-Mill Farm!-- You confined to the society of the illiterate and vulgar all your life!  I wonder how the young man could have the assurance to ask it.  He must have a pretty good opinion of himself."
R373	Addressee Arg1:T272 Arg2:T277	
R374	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T277	
T278	Quotation 73975 74027	"I do not think he is conceited either, in general,"
T279	Cue 74028 74032	said
T280	Entity 74033 74040	Harriet
T281	Quotation 74080 74750	"at least, he is very good natured, and I shall always feel much obliged to him, and have a great regard for-- but that is quite a different thing from-- and you know, though he may like me, it does not follow that I should-- and certainly I must confess that since my visiting here I have seen people-- and if one comes to compare them, person and manners, there is no comparison at all, one is so very handsome and agreeable.  However, I do really think Mr. Martin a very amiable young man, and have a great opinion of him; and his being so much attached to me-- and his writing such a letter-- but as to leaving you, it is what I would not do upon any consideration."
R375	Cueing Arg1:T279 Arg2:T278	
R376	Speaker Arg1:T280 Arg2:T278	
R377	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T278	
R378	Cueing Arg1:T279 Arg2:T281	
R379	Speaker Arg1:T280 Arg2:T281	
R380	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T281	
T282	Quotation 74751 74954	"Thank you, thank you, my own sweet little friend.  We will not be parted.  A woman is not to marry a man merely because she is asked, or because he is attached to her, and can write a tolerable letter."
R381	Addressee Arg1:T280 Arg2:T282	
T283	Entity 75000 75004	Emma
R382	Speaker Arg1:T283 Arg2:T282	
