T305	Quotation 77971 78053	"Let us think of those among our absent friends who are more cheerfully employed,"
R409	Cueing Arg1:T304 Arg2:T305	
R410	Speaker Arg1:T303 Arg2:T305	
R411	Addressee Arg1:T301 Arg2:T305	
T306	Quotation 78067 78304	"At this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is shewing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling how much more beautiful is the original, and after being asked for it five or six times, allowing them to hear your name, your own dear name."
R412	Cueing Arg1:T304 Arg2:T306	
R413	Speaker Arg1:T303 Arg2:T306	
R414	Addressee Arg1:T301 Arg2:T306	
T307	Quotation 78305 78363	"My picture!-- But he has left my picture in Bond-street."
R415	Addressee Arg1:T303 Arg2:T307	
R416	Speaker Arg1:T301 Arg2:T307	
T308	Quotation 78364 78875	"Has he so!-- Then I know nothing of Mr. Elton.  No, my dear little modest Harriet, depend upon it the picture will not be in Bond-street till just before he mounts his horse to-morrow.  It is his companion all this evening, his solace, his delight.  It opens his designs to his family, it introduces you among them, it diffuses through the party those pleasantest feelings of our nature, eager curiosity and warm prepossession.  How cheerful, how animated, how suspicious, how busy their imaginations all are!"
R417	Speaker Arg1:T303 Arg2:T308	
T309	Entity 78876 78883	Harriet
R418	Addressee Arg1:T309 Arg2:T308	
T310	Quotation 79945 80288	"Well, I believe, if you will excuse me, Mr. Knightley, if you will not consider me as doing a very rude thing, I shall take Emma's advice and go out for a quarter of an hour.  As the sun is out, I believe I had better take my three turns while I can.  I treat you without ceremony, Mr. Knightley.  We invalids think we are privileged people."
T311	Entity 79763 79776	Mr. Knightley
T312	Entity 79934 79943	the other
R419	Speaker Arg1:T312 Arg2:T310	
R420	Speaker Arg1:T311 Arg2:T310	
T313	Quotation 80289 80333	"My dear sir, do not make a stranger of me."
R421	Speaker Arg1:T311 Arg2:T313	
R422	Addressee Arg1:T312 Arg2:T313	
T314	Quotation 80334 80509	"I leave an excellent substitute in my daughter.  Emma will be happy to entertain you.  And therefore I think I will beg your excuse and take my three turns-- my winter walk."
R423	Addressee Arg1:T311 Arg2:T314	
R424	Speaker Arg1:T312 Arg2:T314	
T315	Quotation 80510 80538	"You cannot do better, sir."
R425	Speaker Arg1:T311 Arg2:T315	
R426	Addressee Arg1:T312 Arg2:T315	
T316	Quotation 80539 80738	"I would ask for the pleasure of your company, Mr. Knightley, but I am a very slow walker, and my pace would be tedious to you; and, besides, you have another long walk before you, to Donwell Abbey."
R427	Addressee Arg1:T311 Arg2:T316	
R428	Speaker Arg1:T312 Arg2:T316	
T317	Quotation 80739 80903	"Thank you, sir, thank you; I am going this moment myself; and I think the sooner you go the better.  I will fetch your greatcoat and open the garden door for you."
